.TH BPF_COMPILER 8 "August 2026" "Linux" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
bpfc \- Pure C eBPF bytecode compiler and direct network fabric loader
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bpfc
.RB [ \-i
.IR interface ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR direction ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR priority ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-I ]
.RB [ \-T ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR map_dir ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR NAME = value ]
.RB [ \-L
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-E | \-M ]
.RI { "<instructions>" " | " \-f " file" }
.PP
.B bpfc
.RB \-i
.IR interface
.RB \-c
.PP
.B bpfc
.RB \-i
.IR interface
.RB [ \-d
.IR direction ]
.B \-p
.I priority
.B \-D
.PP
.B bpfc
.RB \-i
.IR interface
.B \-d xdp
.RB { \-D | \-c }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bpfc
is a standalone, LLVM/Clang-independent eBPF assembler and loader. It parses a 
custom, semicolon-separated instruction set to generate raw eBPF bytecode, 
loading it directly into the Linux kernel via the \fBbpf()\fR syscall, and attaching 
it to a network interface using direct Netlink messages (bypassing the \fBtc\fR utility).
.PP
The compiler supports advanced packet modification operations up to Layer 4, 
including L3/L4 checksum recalculation, arbitrary byte injection, VLAN/QinQ manipulation, 
and GRE/MPLS encapsulation and decapsulation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-i " interface"
Specifies the target network interface (e.g., eth0) to attach the compiled eBPF program. If omitted, the compiler will only write the generated bytecode to \fBoutput.bpf\fR in the current directory.
.TP
.BI \-d " direction"
Specifies the hook direction for the eBPF filter. Valid options are \fBingress\fR (default), \fBegress\fR and \fBxdp\fR. \fBxdp\fR compiles an XDP program and attaches it as the interface's XDP program; see \fBXDP\fR below.
.TP
.B \-c
Cleanup mode. Atomically detaches all eBPF programs from the specified interface, in both directions, by removing the \fBclsact\fR qdisc. Must be used with \fB\-i\fR. With \fB\-d xdp\fR it removes the interface's XDP program instead, and leaves TC alone.
.TP
.BI \-p " priority"
TC filter priority, 1\-65535; lower priorities run first. If a program from bpfc is already attached at this priority and direction, it is replaced atomically, so traffic is never left unfiltered. If the priority holds a filter that cannot be swapped atomically (another protocol or classifier), bpfc warns and removes it before attaching. Priority 0 (the default) lets the kernel pick a free priority.
.TP
.B \-D
Delete only the filter at \fB\-p\fR \fIpriority\fR on \fB\-d\fR \fIdirection\fR, leaving other priorities and the other direction in place. Succeeds if nothing is attached there. With \fB\-d xdp\fR (no \fB\-p\fR) it removes the interface's XDP program.
.TP
.BI \-n " name"
Name the TC filter \fIname\fR (shown by \fBtc filter show\fR) and the kernel program after the part of \fIname\fR following its last \(oq:\(cq, limited to its last 15 characters of [A\-Za\-z0\-9_.] (other characters become \(oq_\(cq; shown by \fBbpftool prog\fR).
.TP
.B \-I
After attaching, print one line describing the program:
.EX
BPFC_INFO id=<id> tag=<tag> insns=<n> iface=<if> dir=<dir> prio=<p> proto=<0xNNNN> name=<name> [verified=<n>]
.EE
For XDP programs \fBdir\fR is \fBxdp\fR and \fBprio\fR is 0.
\fBverified\fR (kernel 5.16 and later) is the number of instructions the verifier processed; the kernel rejects a program that reaches 1,000,000.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Read instructions from \fIfile\fR (\fB\-\fR for standard input). Line breaks act as spaces, and a \fB#\fR at the start of an instruction begins a comment that ends at the end of the line. Include files are read the same way.
.TP
.BI \-m " map_dir"
Directory for pinned maps (default \fI/sys/fs/bpf\fR). A map is pinned there under its name, which must be 1\-31 characters without \fB/\fR; the kernel's own name for it is the last 15 characters, with characters other than letters, digits, \fB_\fR and \fB.\fR replaced by \fB_\fR.
.TP
.BI \-r " map"
Print the contents of a pinned map and exit.
.TP
.BI \-w " map key value"
Write a 64-bit \fIvalue\fR under \fIkey\fR in a pinned 8-byte map (for example a program's runtime settings) and exit. Numbers may be decimal or 0x hexadecimal.
.TP
.B \-E
Print the program after preprocessing (\fBinclude\fR, \fBconst\fR, \fBdefine\fR, \fBrepeat\fR, \fBwhen\fR and \fB$\fR substitution) as plain instructions, one per line, and exit without compiling.
.TP
.BI \-s " NAME" = value
Set the constant \fINAME\fR, overriding a \fBconst\fR \fINAME\fR in the program. May be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-L " dir"
Search \fIdir\fR for include files, before the default \fI/etc/bpfc/include\fR. May be given more than once.
.TP
.B \-M
Print the program file and every file it includes, one per line, and exit. \fBbpfcd\fR(8) uses this to notice changes to included files.
.TP
.B \-T
Check the program: compile it and load it into the kernel so the verifier
checks it, then exit without attaching it. With
.B \-i
the interface only supplies \fB$IFNAME\fR, \fB$IFINDEX\fR and the other
interface constants; nothing on it changes. Maps that are already pinned in the
map directory are used as they are; maps that do not exist yet are created
without pinning them, and disappear when the check ends; a pinned map whose
definition differs from the program's is left in place (a warning says that
attaching the program would replace it). Needs the same privileges as
attaching. \fBbpfcd\fR(8) uses this to check programs written through its
REST socket before installing them.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose compiler output.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. 1 if the program cannot be compiled, is rejected by the kernel verifier, or cannot be attached or detached; the kernel's reason is printed. Earlier versions could report success when attaching failed.
.SH INSTRUCTION SYNTAX
Instructions are provided as a single string argument, separated by semicolons. 
.SS Matching Rules
Conditional matching acts as a gatekeeper. If a packet fails a match rule, the eBPF program immediately jumps to the epilogue, allowing the packet to pass unmodified.
.TP
.BI "match " "field value"
Compares a specific packet header field to a value. 
Supported fields: \fBsrc-mac\fR, \fBdst-mac\fR, \fBeth-proto\fR, \fBeth-vlan\fR, \fBmpls-label\fR, \fBmpls-bos\fR, \fBip-proto\fR, \fBip-tos\fR, \fBip-src\fR, \fBip-dst\fR, \fBtcp-src\fR, \fBtcp-dst\fR, \fBudp-src\fR, \fBudp-dst\fR.
The TCP/UDP ports, \fBtcp-flags\fR, \fBicmp-type\fR and the GRE fields are found from the real IPv4 header length, so packets with IP options are handled (earlier versions read them at fixed offsets that assume a 20-byte header). On a packet that is not IPv4 such a match does not match, \fBget\fR stores 0 and \fBset\fR changes nothing.
.TP
.BI "match dev-type " "n | %VAR"
.TQ
.BI "match dev-kind " kind
.TQ
.BI "get dev-type " VAR
The device type and kind of the \fB\-i\fR interface (\fBskb->dev->type\fR, which a TC program cannot read). They are looked up when compiling, so the match costs nothing: its block is entered always or never. Without \fB\-i\fR, give \fB\-s IFTYPE=\fIn\fR and \fB\-s IFKIND=\fIkind\fR.
.SS Preprocessor
These are expanded before compilation; \fB\-E\fR shows the result. A program that uses none of them and no \fB$\fR is compiled exactly as written.
.TP
.BI "const " "NAME value"
Define \fB$\fINAME\fR. A number or constant expression is stored as a number, anything else (an address, a MAC, an interface name) as text. \fB\-s\fR overrides it; defining a const twice with different values is an error.
.TP
\fBdefine\fR \fINAME\fR [\fIPARAM\fR ...] ; ... ; \fBend-define\fR
Define a macro, used as an instruction: \fINAME arg ...\fR. In the body, \fB$\fIPARAM\fR is the argument and \fB$UNIQUE\fR a number unique to each use (for labels). The body sees only its parameters and consts. A macro may not be named after a bpfc instruction.
.TP
.BI "include " file
Read \fIfile\fR here, once per program. A relative name is looked up next to the including file, then in the \fB\-L\fR directories, then in \fI/etc/bpfc/include\fR. World-writable files are refused.
.TP
\fBrepeat\fR \fIcount\fR [\fBas\fR \fII\fR] ; ... ; \fBend-repeat\fR
Copy the body \fIcount\fR times (0\-4096) with \fB$\fII\fR = 0, 1, ... The result is straight-line code without backward jumps, so it needs no loop support from the kernel verifier. Every copy adds instructions; bpfc accepts programs of up to 32768.
.TP
\fBrepeat\fR \fI%VAR\fR \fBmax\fR \fIcount\fR [\fBas\fR \fII\fR] ; ... ; \fBend-repeat\fR
As above, but stop once \fI%VAR\fR <= \fB$\fII\fR (checked before each copy): the body runs \fI%VAR\fR times, at most \fIcount\fR.
.TP
.B break-repeat
.TQ
.B next-repeat
Leave the innermost \fBrepeat\fR, or continue with its next copy.
.TP
\fBwhen\fR \fIexpr\fR ; ... ; [\fBotherwise\fR ; ...] ; \fBend-when\fR
Keep the first branch if \fIexpr\fR is not 0, else the \fBotherwise\fR branch. \fBdefined(\fINAME\fB)\fR is 1 for a defined const. \fIword\fR \fB==\fR \fIword\fR (or \fB!=\fR) compares text when a side is not a number, e.g. \fBwhen $IFKIND == gre\fR.
.TP
\fB$L3OFF\fR
Where the IP header starts: 14 behind an Ethernet header, 0 on devices whose packets start with it (device types none, GRE, IP6GRE, IPIP, IPv6 tunnel, SIT, raw IP and PPP: tun, wireguard, gre, ipip, sit, ...). bpfc reads the IP and L4 fields from there by itself, and \fBmatch ip\fR, \fBip6\fR, \fBtcp\fR, ... check the IP version instead of the EtherType. Ethernet fields and commands (MACs, \fBeth-proto\fR, VLAN, ARP, MPLS, \fBpush-eth\fR, \fBpop-eth\fR, the encap and decap commands, \fBstrip-l2\fR, \fBinsert-l2\fR, \fBfib-lookup\fR, \fBip-frag\fR) are an error there. Raw offsets (\fBget bytes\fR, \fBadd-bytes\fR, ...) are frame offsets: write \fB$(L3OFF + \fIn\fB)\fR in a program meant for both. Set by \fB\-i\fR, or \fB\-s L3OFF=0\fR.
.TP
\fB$IFNAME\fR, \fB$IFINDEX\fR, \fB$IFTYPE\fR, \fB$IFKIND\fR
Set from \fB\-i\fR: the interface's name, index, device type (ARPHRD: 1 Ethernet, 772 loopback, 778 GRE, 65534 none) and kind (as \fBip \-d link\fR shows it: veth, gre, vxlan, wireguard, ...; \fBnic\fR for a device without a kind, \fBlo\fR for loopback). \fBbpfcd\fR compiles its \fI%all%\fR and \fI%<kind>%\fR templates once per interface with these set. \fB\-s\fR overrides them.
.TP
\fB$\fINAME\fR, \fB$(\fIexpr\fB)\fR, \fB$$\fR
Replaced anywhere in an instruction by a const, macro parameter or repeat counter, by the value of \fIexpr\fR, or by \fB$\fR. Expressions use C operators and precedence on 64-bit integers (\fB>>\fR is logical); names inside \fB$( )\fR need no \fB$\fR. A name ends at the first character that is not a letter, digit or \fB_\fR, so write \fB$(I)_x\fR rather than \fB$I_x\fR.
.PP
Errors name the file and line. Example: sum four 32-bit words after a variable-length count.
.EX
const WORDS 4;
decl W 4; decl N 4; decl S 8;
get bytes 16 2 N;
repeat %N max $WORDS as I;
  get bytes $(18 + I*4) 4 W;
  calc add S %W;
end-repeat
.EE
.SS L2 / Ethernet Actions
.TP
.BI "set-dst-mac " "mac_address"
.TP
.BI "set-src-mac " "mac_address"
.TP
.BI "push-eth " "dst_mac src_mac"
Pushes a new 14-byte Ethernet header, retaining the original EtherType.
.TP
.BI "push-vlan " "vid [pcp]"
Pushes an IEEE 802.1Q VLAN tag.
.TP
.B "pop-vlan"
Strips the outermost VLAN tag.
.TP
.BI "set-vlan-id " "vid"
Modifies the 12-bit VLAN ID of an existing 802.1Q tag.
.TP
.BI "push-qinq " "outer_vid inner_vid [outer_pcp] [inner_pcp]"
Pushes double nested tags (Outer 802.1ad, Inner 802.1Q).
.SS L3 & L4 Actions (Checksum-Aware)
.TP
.BI "set-ip-src " "ip_address"
.TP
.BI "set-ip-dst " "ip_address"
.TP
.BI "set-ip-tos " "tos_byte"
.TP
.BI "set-ip-protocol " "proto_byte"
.TP
.BI "set-tcp-src " "port"
.TP
.BI "set-tcp-dst " "port"
.TP
.BI "set-udp-src " "port"
.TP
.BI "set-udp-dst " "port"
.SS Tunnels and Encapsulation
.TP
.BI "encap-mpls label " "val" " bos " "val"
Pushes a 4-byte MPLS header immediately following the MAC header.
.TP
.B "decap-mpls"
Strips the 4-byte MPLS header (and a pseudowire control word when one follows)
and sets the EtherType to 0x86DD when IPv6 follows, 0x0800 otherwise.
.TP
.BI "encap-gre ip-src " "ip" " ip-dst " "ip" " key " "val"
Encapsulates the original frame within an Outer IPv4 header and GRE key header.
.TP
.B "decap-gre"
Strips 28 bytes of Outer IPv4 + GRE headers.
.SS Atomic Map Updates
.TP
.BI "calc-map " "op map key value [OLD]"
Applies \fIop\fR (\fBadd\fR, \fBor\fR, \fBand\fR, \fBxor\fR or \fBxchg\fR) atomically to the 64-bit value under \fIkey\fR, creating it as 0 if missing. With \fIOLD\fR (required for \fBxchg\fR) the previous value is stored in that 8-byte variable. Unlike \fBget map\fR / \fBset map\fR, updates from several CPUs at once are never lost. Needs kernel 5.12 (RHEL 9).
.TP
.BI "tcp-option " "kind|%VAR OFF_VAR [LEN_VAR]"
Find the first TCP option of \fIkind\fR (IPv4, any IHL): \fIOFF_VAR\fR gets its frame offset and \fILEN_VAR\fR its length byte, both 0 if absent.
.TP
.BI "fill-bytes " "offset|%VAR len|%VAR byte|%VAR"
Overwrite 1\-64 packet bytes with one byte value. Checksums are not updated (see \fBrecalc-tcp-csum\fR).
.TP
.BI "get " "tcp-seq|tcp-ack|tcp-flags|tcp-win|tcp-hlen|l4-off|payload-off|payload-len VAR"
TCP header fields, the L4 header offset and the payload offset and length, following the real IPv4 and TCP header lengths; 0 when they do not apply.
.TP
.BI "get flow-key " "VAR [reverse|symmetric]"
64-bit hash of the IPv4 addresses, protocol and TCP/UDP ports; \fBreverse\fR gives the key of the opposite direction, \fBsymmetric\fR one key for both.
.TP
.BI "get|set bytes " "%OFF len ..."
A variable offset reads with \fBbpf_skb_load_bytes\fR (0 outside the packet) and writes with \fBbpf_skb_store_bytes\fR.
.TP
.BI "get|set tstamp " "VAR|value"
\fBskb->tstamp\fR in ns; a future time on egress paces the packet under the fq qdisc.
.TP
\fBif\fR \fIA\fR \fBseq-lt\fR|\fBseq-le\fR|\fBseq-gt\fR|\fBseq-ge\fR \fIB\fR
Serial-number comparison of 32-bit wrapping counters (TCP sequence numbers).
.TP
.BI "array-map " "map entries"
Declare \fImap\fR as an array of \fIentries\fR 64-bit values with keys 0..\fIentries\fR\-1, instead of the default LRU hash map of 10000 entries. Entries are never evicted and start at 0; a key out of range reads as 0 and is not stored. Works with \fBget map\fR, \fBset map\fR, \fBcalc-map\fR, \fB\-r\fR and \fB\-w\fR. A pinned map of another size is replaced.
.SS Buffer Maps
.TP
.BI "buffer-map " "map [value_size]"
.TQ
.BI "shared-buffer-map " "map [entries [value_size]]"
Declare a per-CPU (1 entry) or shared buffer map before it is used, with smaller values than the default 18000 bytes (and 10000 entries). A copy at a constant offset in the buffer may fill the rest of the entry; with a variable offset, offset and length are each capped at \fIvalue_size\fR/2. A pinned map of other sizes is replaced; one used without a declaration keeps its own sizes.
.TP
.BI "xor-packet " "map len [src [dst]]"
.TQ
.BI "xor-packet-keyed " "map key len [src [dst]]"
XOR \fIlen\fR bytes of the packet from offset \fIsrc\fR into the buffer at \fIdst\fR (rounded down to a multiple of 8), with 64-bit atomic operations. Bytes past the end of the packet count as zero. Used to build and use XOR parity (forward error correction).
.TP
.BI "zero-buffer " "map len [dst]"
.TQ
.BI "zero-buffer-keyed " "map key len [dst]"
Zero \fIlen\fR bytes (rounded up to a multiple of 8) of a buffer entry.
.SS Checksums
.TP
.BI "recalc-tcp-csum " "[l3_offset [max_len]]"
.TQ
.BI "recalc-udp-csum " "[l3_offset [max_len]]"
Recompute the TCP or UDP checksum of the IPv4 packet whose header starts at \fIl3_offset\fR (default 14) from scratch: any IHL, length taken from the IP header (Ethernet padding is ignored), up to \fImax_len\fR bytes (default 9000; a lower limit makes the program cheaper to verify). Earlier versions assumed a 20-byte header and failed on packets over 512 bytes. Use it on ingress, or on egress only for packets without checksum offload pending: on egress, locally sent TCP/UDP packets usually still carry a partial checksum that the kernel or NIC completes after the program, and a full checksum written there would be summed again.
.TP
.BI "calc-csum " "tcp|udp VAR [l3_offset [max_len]]"
Compute the correct checksum without changing the packet (the current checksum field is ignored) and store it in \fIVAR\fR in host byte order, like \fBget bytes\fR. Useful to check a packet, or to see the checksum a packet will have after checksum offload.
.SS Layer-2 Shims
.TP
.BI "insert-l2 " "len [ethertype]"
Insert \fIlen\fR zero bytes (1\-64) after the Ethernet header with \fBbpf_skb_adjust_room\fR and optionally set the EtherType. Network and transport headers keep their place in memory, so checksum offload still works. Only for IPv4/IPv6 packets.
.TP
.BI "remove-l2 " "len [ethertype]"
The counterpart of \fBinsert-l2\fR: remove \fIlen\fR bytes (1\-64) after the Ethernet header with \fBbpf_skb_adjust_room\fR and optionally set the EtherType. skb->protocol stays IPv4/IPv6, so the packet can be grown again with \fBinsert-l2\fR later (for example after it was cloned or redirected); network and transport headers keep their place in memory. Only for IPv4/IPv6 packets.
.TP
.BI "strip-l2 " "len ethertype"
Remove \fIlen\fR bytes (a multiple of 4, 4\-56) after the Ethernet header and set the EtherType, whatever the packet's protocol (\fBbpf_skb_adjust_room\fR refuses non-IP packets). Uses the VLAN helpers; the payload is not copied, and skb->protocol is updated so the stack accepts the packet directly.
.SS Other
.TP
.BI "get cpu " "VAR"
The number of the CPU running the program, for per-CPU state in ordinary maps.
.TP
.BI "calc hton " "VAR [bits]"
.TQ
.BI "calc ntoh " "VAR [bits]"
Convert between host and network byte order. \fBget bytes\fR returns host order and \fBset bytes\fR with a variable writes it as stored, so convert before writing.
.SS Advanced Actions
.TP
.BI "add-bytes " "offset hex_string"
Writes arbitrary bytes directly into the packet at the specified offset.
.TP
.BI "redirect " "interface"
Forwards the packet directly to another interface (by name or index).
.SH XDP
With \fB\-d xdp\fR the program runs at the interface's XDP hook, before the kernel allocates an skb and before GRO, TC ingress and the stack. An interface has one XDP program, so there is no priority (\fB\-p\fR is refused); attaching replaces the current one atomically. bpfc attaches in generic (skb) mode, which works on every device; native (driver) mode is planned. The kernel refuses while a program is attached in native mode. Generic mode needs kernel 5.18 or RHEL 9.2 (\fBbpf_xdp_load_bytes\fR).
.PP
The commands compile as for TC, with these differences:
.TP
.B accept\fR, \fBcontinue
Pass the packet on (XDP_PASS); TC ingress and the stack see it next.
.TP
.B drop
XDP_DROP.
.TP
.B tx
XDP only: send the packet back out of the interface it arrived on (XDP_TX). Set the MAC addresses first.
.TP
.BI "redirect " interface
Out of \fIinterface\fR (XDP_REDIRECT); \fBingress\fR is refused.
.TP
.B Checksums
\fBset ip-src\fR, ports, \fBip-tos\fR and the other checksum-aware fields update the IP and TCP/UDP checksums incrementally, as on TC (a UDP checksum of 0 stays 0). XDP sees complete checksums from the wire. A packet a local socket sent through a veth pair can still carry a partial checksum (CHECKSUM_PARTIAL), which an L4 rewrite would corrupt: turn tx checksum offload off on such senders (\fBethtool \-K\fR \fIdev\fR \fBtx off\fR).
.TP
.B VLAN
The tag is in the packet: \fBmatch vlan\fR and \fBqinq\fR check the EtherType at offset 12, \fBget\fR/\fBmatch vlan\-id\fR read the TCI at 14, and \fBset vlan\-id\fR rewrites its 12 id bits (priority and DEI stay) or, without a tag, pushes an 802.1Q tag. A device that strips tags in hardware (\fBrxvlan\fR offload) may hand generic XDP packets without them.
.TP
.B get len
The packet length, from \fBbpf_xdp_get_buff_len\fR. \fBget skb\-ingress\fR and \fBskb\-ifindex\fR give the ingress interface, \fBget queue\fR the receive queue.
.TP
.B Resizing
\fBadd\-head\-bytes\fR, \fBadd\-bytes\fR, \fBdel\-bytes\fR, \fBadd\-l2\-bytes\fR, \fBdel\-l2\-bytes\fR, \fBpush\-\fR/\fBpop\-eth\fR, \fBpush\-\fR/\fBpop\-vlan\fR, \fBpush\-qinq\fR, \fBencap\-\fR/\fBdecap\-mpls\fR, \fBencap\-\fR/\fBdecap\-gre\fR, \fBinsert\-l2\fR, \fBremove\-l2\fR, \fBstrip\-l2\fR and \fBset len\fR work, so the MPLS pseudowire examples do too. They grow or shrink the packet at the front with \fBbpf_xdp_adjust_head\fR (at most 255 bytes at a time; generic mode has 256 bytes of headroom) and at the end with \fBbpf_xdp_adjust_tail\fR. Inserted bytes are zero, as on TC. With an offset up to 64, \fBadd\-bytes\fR and \fBdel\-bytes\fR move only the bytes in front of it; growing at the end needs tailroom, which generic mode may not have. VLAN tags are pushed into and popped from the packet (4 bytes at offset 12, the MAC addresses moved in front).
.TP
.B Refused
Commands that need an skb are a compile error naming the command: \fBip\-frag\fR, \fBip\-defrag\fR, \fBclone\fR, \fBredirect\-neigh\fR, \fBreclassify\fR, and the skb fields (\fBskb\-mark\fR, \fBskb\-hash\fR, \fBskb\-cb\fR, \fBprotocol\fR, \fBtstamp\fR, \fBset queue\fR). Maps, buffer maps, \fBxor\-packet\fR, \fBcalc\-csum\fR, \fBfib\-lookup\fR and the rest work.
.PP
The constants \fB$XDP\fR (1 in an XDP program, else 0) and \fB$HOOK\fR (\fBingress\fR, \fBegress\fR or \fBxdp\fR) let one file serve several hooks: \fBwhen $XDP == 0\fR ... \fBend\-when\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Intercept traffic matching a specific destination IP, force a VLAN tag on it, and redirect it to a monitoring interface:
.RS
.B bpfc \-i eth0 \-d ingress "match ip-dst 10.0.0.5; push-vlan 100; redirect eth1"
.RE
.PP
Set up a transparent GRE tunnel on outbound traffic for a specific UDP port:
.RS
.B bpfc \-i eth1 \-d egress "match udp-dst 53; encap-gre ip-src 1.1.1.1 ip-dst 2.2.2.2 key 1024"
.RE
.PP
Remove the eBPF program from eth0 and clean up the TC hook:
.RS
.B bpfc \-i eth0 \-c
.RE
.PP
Drop UDP to port 9 at the XDP hook of eth0, then remove the program:
.RS
.B bpfc \-i eth0 \-d xdp "match udp; match udp-dst 9; drop"
.br
.B bpfc \-i eth0 \-d xdp \-D
.RE
.SH NOTES
The 512-byte eBPF stack holds 128 bytes of fixed instruction scratch, then one 8-byte slot per variable (two for a 16-byte IPv6 address), up to 48 slots. Instructions that need more scratch (\fBfib-lookup\fR 72 bytes, \fBpush-eth\fR 32, byte insertion and deletion) use the space below the variables while they run. \fBxor-packet\fR and the TCP/UDP checksums work in chunks of up to 256 bytes, smaller when more than 16 variables leave less room (more verifier instructions, same result). \fBfib-lookup\fR adds five variables. When something does not fit, bpfc names the instruction and the bytes it needs; \fBfree\fR variables that are no longer used. Arbitrary byte injection (\fBadd-bytes\fR) is constrained to 256 bytes per instruction to satisfy kernel verifier bounds.
